ROLE
You are a senior full-stack engineer and database specialist. Upgrade the existing "ClayStock - Stationery Management System" (React 19 + TypeScript + Vite, Tailwind, claymorphism UI, 3D hero, Express server.ts with Gemini) from a MICRO project into a competition-winning DBMS MINI project. The audience is engineering faculty judging DBMS depth, practical value, innovation and demo quality, against rival projects (SQL-Arena, ResearchBuddy, Smart Bus Ticket Reservation, CampusReady). The goal is to make the DATABASE the hero: every concept taught in a DBMS course should be visibly, demonstrably used in the app on a real SQL engine.

======================================================
HARD RULES
======================================================
1. Keep the existing look: claymorphism design tokens, light and dark themes, fonts, 3D hero, tilt cards, toasts, command palette, tour. New pages must match this style exactly (same Clay* components, same shadows, same contrast rules: light colors with dark text in light mode, dark colors with light text in dark mode, WCAG AA).
2. Keep all five original modules (Item, Stock, Purchase, Issue, Search) and the 10 core tables from the project document; extend, never remove. ISSUED_TO gains EmpID and ReqID columns (nullable) so items can be issued to departments OR employees, as in the project objectives.
3. Do not regress performance. The app previously lagged and showed "Page Unresponsive". Keep all of these: lazy-loaded pages, memoized queries, split contexts, lazy 3D canvas that pauses when off-screen, no box-shadow transitions, no deep-copy of state. If those optimizations are not in the code yet, apply them first. New rule: NO SQL EVER RUNS ON THE MAIN THREAD (see Phase A).
4. All business data must come from SQL, not from JavaScript aggregation. The UI calls named queries/views/procedures; the DB enforces the rules (constraints and triggers). JS only formats results and maps DB error codes to friendly messages.
5. Every executed statement is written to the Live SQL Log with: timestamp, statement, parameters, rows returned/affected, execution time in ms, status, and the user who ran it.
6. Be honest in the UI. Where something is an emulation or simulation (GRANT/REVOKE roles, stored procedures, isolation-level demo), label it with a small "Simulated / Emulated" chip and a tooltip explaining what real MySQL/PostgreSQL would do. Never claim a feature the engine does not truly have.
7. Gemini API stays server-side only (server.ts). Use one GEMINI_MODEL constant. Never expose the key to the client.
8. Work in phases A to I in order. After each phase run npm run lint and npm run build, fix errors, and make sure the app still loads before continuing. End with a change report (files changed, new tables/triggers/views/indexes created, bundle sizes).

======================================================
PHASE A: REAL SQL ENGINE (SQLite via sql.js, in a Web Worker)
======================================================
A1. Install sql.js. Create src/db/worker/db.worker.ts (module worker: new Worker(new URL('./db.worker.ts', import.meta.url), { type: 'module' })). Import the WASM with Vite: import wasmUrl from 'sql.js/dist/sql-wasm.wasm?url' and pass locateFile to initSqlJs. The worker owns the Database object. The main thread talks to it through a small typed RPC client (src/db/client.ts) using postMessage with request IDs and promises.
A2. RPC API: exec(sql, params) returning { columns, rows, rowsAffected, ms }; batch(statements[], { atomic: true }) wrapped in BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK; callProcedure(name, args); explain(sql) returning EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN rows; exportDb() returning the .sqlite bytes; importDb(bytes); resetToSeed(); introspect() returning tables, columns, PKs, FKs, indexes, triggers and views from sqlite_master and PRAGMAs; createSandbox({ rows }) returning a disposable second in-memory DB id; dropSandbox(id).
A3. On every connection run PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON. IMPORTANT: db.export() closes and reopens the database and resets pragmas, so re-run PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON immediately after every export. Set PRAGMA user_version = 2 for the new schema.
A4. Persistence: store the exported .sqlite bytes in IndexedDB (not localStorage), debounced 1 second after the last write, and flushed on visibilitychange (hidden) and pagehide. On startup load from IndexedDB; if missing or invalid, create the schema and seed it.
A5. React integration: a useSql(sql, params, deps) hook plus a dbVersion counter that increments after every successful write. Active-page hooks refetch (debounced 50 ms) when dbVersion changes. Results of the same query and params are cached per dbVersion. Cancel stale responses.
A6. Large result grids (over 100 rows) use windowed rendering (small virtualization, e.g. @tanstack/react-virtual) so the main thread never renders thousands of DOM rows.
A7. Migration: if the old localStorage key claystock_relational_db_v1 exists, show a one-time clay dialog "Import your previous data or start with the new realistic demo dataset?" Import copies the 10 core tables into SQLite. Default is the new demo dataset.
A8. If WASM fails to load, show a friendly error card with a Retry button and the reason (never a white screen).

